Description: Constructs sparse multi-environment trial (MET) designs by
    jointly addressing across-environment treatment allocation and
    within-environment field layout under shared statistical, genetic, and
    logistical constraints. Allocation supports two strategies: a
    random-balanced (M3-inspired) approach that guarantees full treatment
    coverage while tolerating unequal environment sizes, and an equireplicate
    (M4-type) approach that enforces equal replication and equal environment
    size, with optional refinement toward a near-balanced (regular-graph)
    design (after Montesinos-Lopez et al. 2023 <doi:10.1002/tpg2.20305>; a
    strict balanced incomplete block design generally does not exist when
    treatments greatly outnumber environments). Optional common treatments
    establish cross-environment genetic connectivity. Structure-aware
    allocation is available via family labels or groups derived from genomic or
    pedigree relationship matrices. Feasibility helpers verify per-environment
    capacity and report strict-BIBD existence conditions. Seed-constrained
    replication plans account for available seed quantities. Within-environment
    layouts are built for repeated-check augmented, partially replicated
    (p-rep), and RCBD-type block designs, or for fixed-grid alpha row-column
    stream designs, with mixed-model efficiency evaluation under A-optimality,
    D-optimality, and CDmean criteria for genomic selection (Rincent et al. 2012
    <doi:10.1534/genetics.112.141473>). A coupled across-environment information
    matrix and a genotype-by-environment simulation report the prediction
    reliability, selection accuracy, and genetic gain of a design, and
    decision-point helpers select representative environments and individuals,
    optimise entry allocation for genomic prediction, and recommend replication
    under resource constraints. Design optimisation is available via random
    restart, simulated annealing, and genetic algorithm methods. An end-to-end
    pipeline coordinates allocation, seed feasibility checks, local field design
    construction, and assembly of a unified MET field book.
